				<div id="bodyContent" class="vector-body" aria-labelledby="firstHeading" data-mw-ve-target-container>
					<div class="vector-body-before-content">
							<div class="mw-indicators">
		</div>

						<div id="siteSub" class="noprint">From Simple English Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia</div>
						<div id="contentSub"><div id="mw-content-subtitle"></div></div>
						
					</div>
					
					<div id="mw-content-text" class="mw-body-content"><div class="mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang="en" dir="ltr" data-mw-parsoid-version="0.24.0.0-alpha24" data-mw-html-version="2.8.0"><section data-mw-section-id="0" id="mwAQ"><figure class="mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAg"><a href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Phobos_deimos_diff.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAw"><img resource="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Phobos_deimos_diff.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e8/Phobos_deimos_diff.jpg?utm_source=simple.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled" decoding="async" loading="lazy" data-file-width="250" data-file-height="398" data-file-type="bitmap" height="239" width="150" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBA"/></a><figcaption id="mwBQ">Phobos (top) and Deimos (bottom).</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBg"><b id="mwBw"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mars" title="Mars" id="mwCA">Mars</a></b> has two little <b id="mwCQ"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite_(natural)" title="Satellite (natural)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCg">moons</a></b> called <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phobos_(moon)" title="Phobos (moon)" id="mwCw">Phobos</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deimos_(moon)" title="Deimos (moon)" id="mwDA">Deimos</a>. They are thought to be captured <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asteroids" title="Asteroids" class="mw-redirect" id="mwDQ">asteroids</a>.<sup about="#mwt3" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-1"}}'><a href="#cite_note-1" id="mwDg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwDw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEA">[</span>1<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwEQ">]</span></span></a></sup> They were found by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asaph_Hall" title="Asaph Hall" id="mwEg">Asaph Hall</a> in 1877.<sup about="#mwt6" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-2"}}'><a href="#cite_note-2" id="mwEw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwFA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFQ">[</span>2<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwFg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<meta property="mw:PageProp/toc" id="mwFw" data-mw='{"autoGenerated":true}'/></section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwGA" aria-labelledby="History"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="History">History</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;veaction=edit&amp;section=1" title="Change section: History" class="mw-editsection-visualeditor"><span>change</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-divider"> | </span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1" title="Edit section's source code: History"><span>change source</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>

<section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwGQ" aria-labelledby="Discovery"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Discovery">Discovery</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;veaction=edit&amp;section=2" title="Change section: Discovery" class="mw-editsection-visualeditor"><span>change</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-divider"> | </span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2" title="Edit section's source code: Discovery"><span>change source</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwGg">The two moons of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mars" title="Mars" id="mwGw">Mars</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phobos_(moon)" title="Phobos (moon)" id="mwHA">Phobos</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deimos_(moon)" title="Deimos (moon)" id="mwHQ">Deimos</a>, were discovered by the American astronomer <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asaph_Hall" title="Asaph Hall" id="mwHg">Asaph Hall</a> in August 1877. He found them at the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Naval_Observatory?action=edit&amp;redlink=1" title="United States Naval Observatory (not yet started)" class="new" typeof="mw:LocalizedAttrs" id="mwHw" data-mw-i18n='{"title":{"lang":"x-page","key":"red-link-title","params":["United States Naval Observatory"]}}'>United States Naval Observatory</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington,_D.C." title="Washington, D.C." id="mwIA">Washington, D.C.</a>,<sup about="#mwt9" class="mw-ref reference" id='cite_ref-Campbell_"Beginning"_3-0' rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Campbell \"Beginning\""},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Campbell_\"Beginning\"-3"}}'><a href='#cite_note-Campbell_"Beginning"-3' id="mwIQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwIg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwIw">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwJA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt12" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:0_4-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":0"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:0-4"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:0-4" id="mwJQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwJg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwJw">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwKA">]</span></span></a></sup> using a large refracting telescope during a close approach of Mars to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth" title="Earth" id="mwKQ">Earth</a>.</p>

<p id="mwKg">Hall discovered Deimos first on 12 August 1877, followed by Phobos on 18 August 1877.<sup about="#mwt13" class="mw-ref reference" id='cite_ref-Campbell_"Beginning"_3-1' rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Campbell \"Beginning\""}}'><a href='#cite_note-Campbell_"Beginning"-3' id="mwKw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwLA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLQ">[</span>3<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwLg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt14" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:0_4-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":0"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:0-4" id="mwLw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwMA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMQ">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwMg">]</span></span></a></sup> He had been carefully searching for moons around Mars, as it had long been suspected that the planet might have satellites, but none had been confirmed before.<sup about="#mwt17" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-5"}}'><a href="#cite_note-5" id="mwMw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwNA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNQ">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwNg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwNw">The moons are very small and orbit close to Mars, which makes them difficult to see because they are often hidden in the planet’s bright glare.<sup about="#mwt20" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-6" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-6"}}'><a href="#cite_note-6" id="mwOA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwOQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwOg">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwOw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt23" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-7" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-7"}}'><a href="#cite_note-7" id="mwPA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwPQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwPg">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwPw">]</span></span></a></sup> By carefully tracking their movement over several nights, Hall was able to confirm that they were natural satellites orbiting Mars.<sup about="#mwt26" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-8" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-8"}}'><a href="#cite_note-8" id="mwQA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwQQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQg">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwQw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="3" id="mwRA" aria-labelledby="Names_and_meaning"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Names_and_meaning">Names and meaning</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;veaction=edit&amp;section=3" title="Change section: Names and meaning" class="mw-editsection-visualeditor"><span>change</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-divider"> | </span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3" title="Edit section's source code: Names and meaning"><span>change source</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwRQ">The moons of Mars, Phobos and Deimos, are named after figures from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greek_mythology" title="Greek mythology" id="mwRg">Greek mythology</a>. They are the sons of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ares" title="Ares" id="mwRw">Ares</a>, the Greek god of war (known as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mars_(mythology)" title="Mars (mythology)" id="mwSA">Mars</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_mythology" title="Roman mythology" id="mwSQ">Roman mythology</a>), who often accompanied him into battle.<sup about="#mwt29" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:1_9-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:1-9"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:1-9" id="mwSg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwSw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwTA">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwTQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwTg">The name <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phobos" title="Phobos" id="mwTw">Phobos</a> means “fear” or “panic”, while <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deimos" title="Deimos" id="mwUA">Deimos</a> means “terror” or “dread”.<sup about="#mwt32" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-10" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-10"}}'><a href="#cite_note-10" id="mwUQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwUg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUw">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVA">]</span></span></a></sup> In mythology, they were seen as personifications of the emotions that soldiers feel during war.</p>

<p id="mwVQ">Both names were suggested shortly after their discovery by Asaph Hall in 1877.<sup about="#mwt35" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-11" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-11"}}'><a href="#cite_note-11" id="mwVg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwVw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWA">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The suggestion came from the British schoolmaster <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_George_Madan?action=edit&amp;redlink=1" title="Henry George Madan (not yet started)" class="new" typeof="mw:LocalizedAttrs" id="mwWg" data-mw-i18n='{"title":{"lang":"x-page","key":"red-link-title","params":["Henry George Madan"]}}'>Henry Madan</a>, who based them on a passage from Homer’s <i id="mwWw"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iliad" title="Iliad" id="mwXA">Iliad</a></i>, where Ares calls upon Fear and Terror.<sup about="#mwt36" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:1_9-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":1"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:1-9" id="mwXQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwXg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwXw">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwYA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mwYQ" aria-labelledby="Physical_characteristics"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Physical_characteristics">Physical characteristics</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;veaction=edit&amp;section=4" title="Change section: Physical characteristics" class="mw-editsection-visualeditor"><span>change</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-divider"> | </span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4" title="Edit section's source code: Physical characteristics"><span>change source</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwYg"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phobos_(moon)" title="Phobos (moon)" id="mwYw">Phobos</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deimos_(moon)" title="Deimos (moon)" id="mwZA">Deimos</a> are much smaller than Earth's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon" title="Moon" id="mwZQ">Moon</a>.<sup about="#mwt39" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:2_12-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:2-12"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:2-12" id="mwZg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwZw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwaA">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwaQ">]</span></span></a></sup> They are not round like most large <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_satellite" title="Natural satellite" id="mwag">moons</a>. Instead, they have irregular shapes because their <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravity" title="Gravity" id="mwaw">gravity</a> is too weak to pull them into <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sphere" title="Sphere" id="mwbA">spheres</a>.<sup about="#mwt42" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-13" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-13"}}'><a href="#cite_note-13" id="mwbQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwbg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwbw">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt45" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:3_14-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":3"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:3-14"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:3-14" id="mwcQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwcg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcw">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwdA">]</span></span></a></sup> Both moons have many <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crater" title="Crater" id="mwdQ">craters</a> and are covered with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dust" title="Dust" id="mwdg">dust</a> and loose <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)" title="Rock (geology)" id="mwdw">rocks</a>.<sup about="#mwt48" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-15" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-15"}}'><a href="#cite_note-15" id="mweA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mweQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mweg">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwew">]</span></span></a></sup> They are also very dark and reflect only a small amount of sunlight. </p>

<p id="mwfA">Phobos is the larger of the two moons. It is about 22 kilometres (14 miles) across, while Deimos is about 12 kilometres (7.5 miles) across.<sup about="#mwt49" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:2_12-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":2"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:2-12" id="mwfQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwfg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfw">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwgA">]</span></span></a></sup> Both moons are thought to be made mostly of rock with materials that contain carbon, making them similar in appearance to some asteroids.<sup about="#mwt52" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-16" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-16"}}'><a href="#cite_note-16" id="mwgQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwgg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwgw">[</span>16<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhA">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwhQ">Scientists believe that the inside of both moons may contain many empty spaces, making them less dense than solid rock.<sup about="#mwt55" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-17" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-17"}}'><a href="#cite_note-17" id="mwhg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwhw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwiA">[</span>17<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwiQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt58" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-18" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-18"}}'><a href="#cite_note-18" id="mwig"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwiw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjA">[</span>18<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Their surfaces are covered by a thick layer of fine dust, called <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regolith" title="Regolith" id="mwjg">regolith</a>, which was created by meteorite impacts over millions of years.<sup about="#mwt61" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-19" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-19"}}'><a href="#cite_note-19" id="mwjw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwkA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkQ">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="5" id="mwkw" aria-labelledby="Orbits_and_motions"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Orbits_and_motions">Orbits and motions</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;veaction=edit&amp;section=5" title="Change section: Orbits and motions" class="mw-editsection-visualeditor"><span>change</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-divider"> | </span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5" title="Edit section's source code: Orbits and motions"><span>change source</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwlA">Phobos and Deimos both orbit Mars in nearly circular paths and travel close to the planet's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equator" title="Equator" id="mwlQ">equator</a>.<sup about="#mwt64" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-20" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-20"}}'><a href="#cite_note-20" id="mwlg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwlw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwmA">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwmQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Like Earth's Moon, they are <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tidal_locking" title="Tidal locking" id="mwmg">tidally locked</a>, which means they always keep the same side facing Mars.<sup about="#mwt67" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-21" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-21"}}'><a href="#cite_note-21" id="mwmw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwnA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwnQ">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwng">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwnw">Phobos is much closer to Mars than Deimos.<sup about="#mwt68" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:3_14-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":3"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:3-14" id="mwoA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwoQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwog">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwow">]</span></span></a></sup> It orbits the planet about once every 7.7 hours, making it the fastest-known <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_satellite" title="Natural satellite" id="mwpA">natural satellite</a> in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://simple.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_System" title="Solar System" id="mwpQ">Solar System</a> in relation to the rotation of its planet.<sup about="#mwt71" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:4_22-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":4"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:4-22"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:4-22" id="mwpg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwpw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqA">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Because it moves around Mars faster than Mars rotates, Phobos appears to rise in the west and set in the east.<sup about="#mwt74" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-23" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-23"}}'><a href="#cite_note-23" id="mwqg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwqw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwrA">[</span>23<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwrQ">]</span></span></a></sup> It can cross the sky in about four hours and may be seen twice during a Martian day.</p>

<p id="mwrg">Deimos is farther from Mars and takes about 30.3 hours to complete one orbit.<sup about="#mwt75" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:3_14-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":3"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:3-14" id="mwrw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwsA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsQ">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt76" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:4_22-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":4"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:4-22" id="mwsw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwtA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtQ">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtg">]</span></span></a></sup> Since it orbits more slowly than Mars rotates, it appears to rise in the east and set in the west, like Earth's Moon.<sup about="#mwt79" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-24" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-24"}}'><a href="#cite_note-24" id="mwtw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwuA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwuQ">[</span>24<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwug">]</span></span></a></sup> However, because its orbit is only slightly longer than a Martian day, it moves very slowly across the sky.</p>

<p id="mwuw">Phobos is gradually moving closer to Mars because of tidal forces. Scientists think that, in the distant future, it may break apart and form a ring around Mars or crash into the planet.<sup about="#mwt82" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-25" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-25"}}'><a href="#cite_note-25" id="mwvA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwvQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwvg">[</span>25<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwvw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt85" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-26" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-26"}}'><a href="#cite_note-26" id="mwwA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwwQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwg">[</span>26<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwww">]</span></span></a></sup> Deimos is slowly moving farther away from Mars.<sup about="#mwt88" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-27" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-27"}}'><a href="#cite_note-27" id="mwxA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwxQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwxg">[</span>27<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwxw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="6" id="mwyA" aria-labelledby="Moons"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Moons">Moons</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;veaction=edit&amp;section=6" title="Change section: Moons" class="mw-editsection-visualeditor"><span>change</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-divider"> | </span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Moons_of_Mars&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6" title="Edit section's source code: Moons"><span>change source</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
